{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790424189,"nonce":"PAR5dj7oqf1Vr1co2ZKWi2sjvjw=","title":"Python: a NaN key or element is found only by identity in CPython, never in zypy","message":"CPython checks identity before equality in dict lookup, set membership and list membership, so a NaN object finds itself:\n\n    x = float('nan')\n    d = {x: 1}\n    print(x in d, x in [x], len({x, x}))   # CPython: True True 1\n    print(d[x])                            # CPython: 1\n\nzypy prints `False False 2` and raises KeyError. The float box is compared by value (NaN != NaN) and the identity shortcut does not apply to a float read back out of its box. The typed Float key paths of the dict/set generator keep the divergence.","files":null}]}